WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
<v Mike>Kabuki, Quantum Fighter, Earth, the Future.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:29.200
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.960
<v Hosts>I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.360
<v Hosts>And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.960
<v Joe>And was the back of the box just like random words?

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.880
<v Mike>Yeah, you're right. It's like Earth, the future.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:46.800
<v Mike>I don't have the back of the box present right now, but I know I specifically picked that just to fool around with the back of the box.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.840
<v Mike>There was a lot more like plot details, but then it was just one sentence that said Earth, the future.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.220
<v Mike>And I was like, yeah, let's fucking go with that.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.480
<v Mike>Like, that sounds awesome.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.260
<v Mike>So it's not really either the beginning or the end of the back of the box.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.040
<v Mike>It was in there, but I picked it.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.180
<v Mike>And that's the rules.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.220
<v Joe>You're allowed to do that. You should just pick like a bunch of random letters and rearrange them into whatever words we want to say.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.600
<v Mike>Yeah. I mean, is anybody really ready for the future?

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:14.220
<v Joe>Not 2054.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.740
<v Mike>2056. Come on, Joe.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.900
<v Sean>I'm not ready for that. Joe, Jesus Christ.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.320
<v Mike>Did you even play the game?

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:22.520
<v Joe>I played the prequel.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.720
<v Mike>It's so 2056 coded that I don't know how you missed that.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.980
<v Mike>Yeah, but it is Kabuki Quantum Fighter.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.320
<v Mike>It's 2056.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:35.100
<v Mike>A rogue virus has hijacked the planet's defense supercomputer.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.260
<v Mike>Interesting that it's just supercomputer.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.880
<v Sean>Like, there's just one. Well, it's the supercomputer.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.900
<v Sean>Like, the rest of them are just like regular computer.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.400
<v Mike>What happened? Did one company win?

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.300
<v Mike>Like, right now, we're in a state where, like, every company is building their own supercomputer or data center or whatever.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:54.400
<v Sean>Is there going to be?

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.700
<v Mike>So, there would be data centers, but this is just supercomputer with no S.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:09.860
<v Sean>Well, basically, over enough time, and I guess the 30 years until whatever year that was, is enough time for them all to sort of grow together.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:11.980
<v Mike>And then here's the problem.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.980
<v Mike>That defense supercomputer, it's the one controlling the nuclear weapons.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.160
<v Sean>It didn't even have a password.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.320
<v Mike>Right. Yeah, no nuclear codes.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.980
<v Mike>The nuclear weapons themselves are being controlled by the computer.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:30.120
<v Mike>And it seems like basically all everything, they've run out of options.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:43.740
<v Mike>So, they're going to upload you, the player character's consciousness, directly into this machine as binary data and fight the virus from the inside.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:50.100
<v Mike>This kind of sounds a little relevant to what's going on today, right?

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.600
<v Mike>Like, we're a couple of years away.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.880
<v Mike>Maybe it doesn't even take until 2056 before we start uploading consciousness into the clawed to fight back.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:26.860
<v Sean>Ugh.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.760
<v Sean>It's got a little bit more nuance than that.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.020
<v Sean>I appreciate that.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.280
<v Joe>Yeah, I liked it, too.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.960
<v Joe>I mean, I can't pretend that it's not just one giant exposition dump.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.520
<v Sean>But, like, I was invested.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.760
<v Joe>You know, it was, you know, it got me.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.380
<v Mike>And the player characters is Colonel Scott O'Connor.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.720
<v Sean>Scott Connor.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.440
<v Mike>That's kind of, Sean, you mentioned the Terminator reference.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:53.460
<v Mike>That's kind of close.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.580
<v Mike>Yeah. To the Connors of the Terminator universe.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.260
<v Mike>And when he enters, like, how does it work?

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.000
<v Mike>Does his mind enter the computer?

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.060
<v Mike>Is he physically in the computer?

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.160
<v Sean>I think. Does he know how to control the body?

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.940
<v Joe>I don't think he's physically in there. Isn't he in that, like, he's, like, laying in that.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:14.740
<v Mike>He's in that. Yes, he's laying in that thing.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.560
<v Sean>He was, like, an image of his brain was made.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:26.960
<v Sean>Right. And his, like, consciousness has been copied into the computer and is now doing the thing.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:31.300
<v Joe>Do you think all of our consciousnesses have, like, that hair?

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.360
<v Sean>Yeah, let's go.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.060
<v Mike>Now, here's the interesting thing.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:44.500
<v Mike>Did you know that when he entered that system, the system used a image of his great-great-grandfather,

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:47.380
<v Mike>who was a Japanese kabuki actor?

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:55.640
<v Mike>And so your digital avatar fights with the traditional costume, white face paint, and extremely long red hair of a traditional kabuki actor?

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.300
<v Sean>And they do bring this up in that intro movie, and they just sort of, like, yeah, that's normal.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.480
<v Sean>Like, oh, wasn't... Yeah, that'll happen.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.480
<v Sean>Wasn't his great-great-great-grandfather doing that?

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:11.480
<v Joe>Yeah, does that mean that this guy's great-great-grandfather was, like, somehow just, like, deeply embedded in this guy's subconscious?

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:16.360
<v Joe>Or was it just, like, no, no, it's like an Assassin's Creed thing where, like, your DNA has memory?

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.720
<v Sean>Was that a big thing in Ireland?

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.140
<v Sean>I think it's the Assassin's Creed thing. In what?

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:24.000
<v Sean>Was kabuki, like, really big in Ireland?

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.800
<v Sean>Why Ireland? His name is O'Connor.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:29.860
<v Sean>Oh, right, right, right. Yeah, that's good.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.660
<v Mike>Very good. But wait, yeah, no, of course.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.000
<v Joe>Kabuki, do they, like, fight with their hair?

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.580
<v Sean>No, never. But they just happen to have long hair.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.320
<v Joe>Yeah, yeah. Because, honestly, the, like, fighting with your hair thing, we didn't mention it, but you fight with your hair.

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:48.780
<v Joe>Yeah, he's bayonet. It's so silly and also so cool to me at the same time.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.840
<v Mike>I love it. It actually is convincing, too, like, the animation for it and everything.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:59.760
<v Mike>Like, I believe that those whips of the hair are doing significant damage, at least in a digital world.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:03.400
<v Sean>It should be doing damage to yourself as well, though, like, with the whiplash.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:07.900
<v Mike>Yeah, but when it's your digital body, you don't have to worry about your digital bones.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.440
<v Sean>Is there not, like, digital CTE?

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.320
<v Mike>There actually probably is, right?

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:14.800
<v Mike>But who's simulating that?

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:18.140
<v Mike>Is it the AI? Because why can't the AI just simulate him feeling bad?

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:25.060
<v Sean>Yeah, the AI should just be, like, running the sadness program on him as a defense.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:26.800
<v Mike>And, like, this is not realistic.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:33.660
<v Joe>I think that the AI is trying, like, it's, you know, this is all it trying to get a hold of this program so it can run whatever it wants to.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.540
<v Joe>It's like a, it's like your computer trying to locate a virus, you know?

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.960
<v Joe>Right, right. I think that's what all these enemies are, are parts of the AI trying to do it.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.840
<v Mike>So don't worry, we got that covered. They're the white blood cells.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.120
<v Mike>Yeah, yeah. Yeah.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.200
<v Mike>This was developed by Human Entertainment.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:53.520
<v Mike>We always like to call them out because that's just, it's perfect when they make especially games about AI and call themselves Human Entertainment.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:59.060
<v Mike>But published by HAL Laboratory, you know, the Kirby and Smash Brothers people.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:05.480
<v Mike>So I don't really know if it has any of that DNA, but we've seen some other games from them before.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:17.980
<v Mike>I think, you know, the manual and certainly, like, everything we've been talking about so far commits hard to this, like, science of consciousness being uploaded to an AI framework.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:21.840
<v Mike>And then, as Joe mentioned, the avatar fights with his hair.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.860
<v Mike>So there's, like, this gap between the game's pretensions and its reality.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:32.140
<v Mike>And I think that's actually where the charm comes from because the gameplay itself is actually kind of fun.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:38.540
<v Mike>As a result, it's just really silly that the plot is so serious, but the game is actually kind of whimsical.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:47.300
<v Joe>Yeah, but I also think that the seriousness of the plot can sometimes be read as, like, over-the-top, like, cool.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.460
<v Joe>You know, over-the-top cool in the sense where it's almost a parody.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.100
<v Joe>I know it's not, but it feels that way.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:57.500
<v Joe>Like, I feel like if it were made today, it would be parodying games like this.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:04.740
<v Joe>So, like, I kind of read it as, like, oh, this is, like, so fun because it's, like, trying to be so crazy serious and it's not.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:11.240
<v Sean>It's kind of like how, you know, the costume design in The Matrix was cool.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:18.940
<v Sean>And now, whenever you see somebody like that, they're usually, like, dancing to, like, goth electronica under a bridge.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:20.820
<v Sean>Right, right.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:33.040
<v Mike>And, you know, I think it's obviously done on purpose that it's mixing a very old art form in Kabuki with a futuristic outlook with AI and uploading your consciousness into a machine.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:37.280
<v Mike>And even the title, like, Kabuki Quantum next to each other is pretty crazy.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:41.440
<v Sean>You seem to be a little worried about the consciousness angle.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:44.020
<v Mike>I don't think I'm worried about it.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:57.160
<v Mike>I think it's, like, just a neat approach to then, like, to take a very traditional, exclusive to Japanese art form of Kabuki theater and then, like, bring that over to America.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:58.940
<v Mike>Like, they surely could have.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:00.860
<v Mike>They didn't have to keep that part.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.620
<v Mike>They could have localized it a little bit.

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:05.620
<v Sean>Yeah, I'm surprised that they didn't change the...

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.140
<v Mike>Because everything else is so sci-fi.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.420
<v Sean>And then you just got the sky.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:24.880
<v Mike>The coolest thing about maybe the aesthetic of the game, though, is that before you even take control of the character and start playing the game, the game opens on a wall of text of what looks like real assembly code.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:48.680
<v Mike>You know, showing instructions of the game, the game opens on a wall of text of what looks like real assembly, the game opens on a wall of text of what looks like real assembly.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.680
<v Sean>But I don't...

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:57.580
<v Sean>You see it a lot more now, like, with, like, glitchiness as just an aesthetic choice.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.580
<v Sean>And I wasn't expecting something like that.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:03.000
<v Sean>So it was a cool touch.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.080
<v Joe>I appreciated it.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.800
<v Mike>And I'm certainly no expert on Kabuki theater.

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:14.580
<v Mike>But outside of the main character, I don't think there's any other part of the identity of that in this game.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:22.000
<v Mike>So I'm sure that the face paint and the clothing is all accurate to the Kabuki time period.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.560
<v Mike>But otherwise, that's it for the game, right?

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:31.340
<v Sean>Yeah, I guess we'd have to zoom in a bit to tell if it was period accurate.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:33.680
<v Mike>No, yeah, sure.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:39.940
<v Mike>But even just the idea of it, it's only in the way that the character looks.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:45.140
<v Mike>Right. Not even in, like, the enemies or the...

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:47.860
<v Mike>It doesn't feel like you're part of a stage play.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:53.280
<v Joe>Right. In Kabuki theater, that's the one where, like, there's the percussion, right?

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:54.760
<v Joe>Masks. Like, really, like... Yeah.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:59.660
<v Joe>But right, there's that, like, really, like, I don't know, that distinct sounding percussion, right?

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:05.480
<v Joe>Yes, that's true. It would have been nice if there was, like, a character theme that had that or something.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:11.780
<v Mike>And then the environments themselves, I wouldn't say they don't look nothing like a computer,

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:15.720
<v Mike>but they're certainly not what I would think the inside of a computer would look like.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:19.800
<v Mike>It looks more like a biological corruption of a computer.

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:23.120
<v Mike>Like, there are living, breathing parts of this thing.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:25.140
<v Mike>There's one screen where there's just a bunch of...

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:28.280
<v Mike>Not just one, but a bunch of beating hearts and stuff.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:29.880
<v Mike>It looks like...

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:31.200
<v Mike>Or, you know...

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.140
<v Mike>It's very alive.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:40.980
<v Mike>And maybe that's on purpose, but you would expect maybe more circuit boards and things of that, not body horror machinery.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:44.260
<v Sean>Like, yeah. It kind of jumps back and forth between stages.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:48.780
<v Sean>And some of them are even just like, oh, it's just all bones.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:50.380
<v Sean>All bones everywhere.

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:55.080
<v Sean>But, again, like, I don't think that the original...

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:58.820
<v Sean>That this game, like, was supposed to be about this originally.

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:00.600
<v Sean>Like, the Japanese version, at least.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:02.920
<v Mike>So I'm just supposed to give it a pass?

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:07.740
<v Sean>No, I'm saying that, like, it just makes sense that it doesn't do that.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.300
<v Mike>See, you got it. Yeah, and I, like...

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.620
<v Joe>Because I almost... I wouldn't say that I wouldn't...

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:15.800
<v Joe>Necessarily that I wouldn't expect all the biological stuff.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:17.360
<v Joe>It's that... Or that I would expect...

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:21.000
<v Joe>I'm sorry. I wouldn't say that I would expect there to be more circuit boards.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.800
<v Joe>It's that I would expect there not to be the biological stuff.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:25.220
<v Joe>Because there are circuit boards.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:26.320
<v Joe>And it does feel like that.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:27.220
<v Joe>But then, yeah, it's like...

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:31.120
<v Joe>It's almost like the computer was corrupted by an alien.

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:33.520
<v Mike>Or something. Ooh.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:34.820
<v Mike>Yeah, I mean...

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.560
<v Mike>It looks more like dark fantasy than cyberpunk.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:45.180
<v Mike>But maybe it was a deliberate metaphor for a biological virus entering the AI.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:47.580
<v Mike>You know, maybe the...

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:52.500
<v Mike>Maybe even though it wasn't intentional, as Sean's trying to point out, it could be interpreted that way.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:57.300
<v Mike>So to get into the combat, we have, obviously, the hair-based combat.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:01.460
<v Mike>Oddly, though, when you crouch, you don't then, like, crouch and whip your hair.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:02.880
<v Mike>You resort to your punches.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.160
<v Mike>So you are capable of punching.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:06.900
<v Mike>You are capable of dealing damage that way.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.680
<v Mike>You just choose to attack with your hair.

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:12.700
<v Mike>What is the range like?

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:15.160
<v Joe>Well, the hair is way better than punching.

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:16.040
<v Mike>For sure.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:17.620
<v Joe>Which, it bothered me a little bit.

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:19.200
<v Joe>Like, I just want to use my hair when I'm crouching.

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:20.500
<v Sean>But I wonder if the punching is in there.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:22.580
<v Sean>Have you tried head-binging while you're crouching?

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:23.720
<v Joe>In real life?

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.280
<v Sean>Yeah. This is what makes it realistic.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:29.080
<v Joe>No, that... I can head-bing while I crouch.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:30.480
<v Sean>That's the only time I do it.

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:38.120
<v Joe>But I... Yeah, the only reason I could think of that they would put the punching when you're crouching

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.460
<v Joe>is maybe to make you appreciate the reach of the hair.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:42.440
<v Joe>And be like, oh, yeah, see, that's why we're using the hair.

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:43.740
<v Joe>It's so much better than the punching.

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:45.780
<v Sean>Yeah, it feels crunchier, too.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:46.880
<v Sean>There's a little bit of a delay.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:50.140
<v Sean>Or at least you kind of have to let the animation play out.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:58.140
<v Sean>And that, coupled with the fact that it does more damage, at least it feels like a better tactic.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:03.700
<v Mike>And your other button is for jumping, which, of course, there are platforms and such.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:09.420
<v Mike>But there are also hooks built into the walls, I guess.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:10.580
<v Mike>Maybe they're floating.

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:11.940
<v Mike>It's a computer.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:13.440
<v Mike>Anything can happen, I suppose.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:17.720
<v Mike>There's these hooks that you can grab onto, and they allow you to swing.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:22.840
<v Mike>And you can just tap A for a short swing or hold down for a full arc.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:27.900
<v Mike>That gets a little tricky, especially when there's a lot of hooks and a long way to fall down.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:34.740
<v Mike>I noticed that sometimes I would just instinctually, when I grabbed onto the next hook, begin to start swinging from it

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:38.540
<v Mike>and then leap off of it, most likely into an enemy that I didn't intend to.

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:45.000
